2. WHAT IS AND IS NOT NOW CLOSED
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
REAL_DENSE_ATTN_Q4_K   = REFUTED   (5 windows across 3 layers)
REAL_DENSE_FFN_UP_Q4_K = REFUTED   (3 layers)
REAL_DENSE_FFN_DN_Q6_K = REFUTED   (3 layers)
REAL_MOE_EXPERT_*      = NOT MEASURED  <-- see section 4

Q4_K vs Q6_K, side by side:
  Q4_K order0 entropy  7.2035 .. 7.2218
  Q6_K order0 entropy  6.8559 .. 6.8777
Q6_K's delta is ~0.34 bits/elem cheaper, which is the expected direction -- a
better base leaves a smaller-magnitude error. But 6.86 bits/elem is still far
above anything worth storing, and the rank-99 cost is UNCHANGED at ~49
bits/elem. A better base reduces the delta's magnitude and its entropy slightly
while leaving its rank structure exactly as hopeless.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. THE STABILITY IS THE FINDING
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
A single window of a single tensor is an anecdote. Eleven cases across three
tensor families (attention projection, FFN up-projection, FFN down-projection),
three layers (0 / 31 / 63), two window origins, and two quant types land inside:

  L1 ratio   0.784 .. 0.802   (2.3% spread)
  entropy    6.86  .. 7.22    (5.2% spread, and that is across TWO quant types)
  rank-99    188    .. 197    (never lower)

The spread is smaller than the difference between any two configurations. The
delta's structure is a property of the weight class, not of the sample.

Rank 99% costs 47-49.25 bits/element. The tensor it is compressing is 32 bits.
The low-rank representation of the low-precision residual is roughly 1.5x
LARGER THAN THE ORIGINAL WEIGHTS, at every accuracy target that matters.

=> On dense Q4_K and Q6_K weights, a ternary-base residual overlay is not a
   compression scheme. Its correction field is neither sparse (2-13%), nor low
   entropy (6.9-7.2 b/elem), nor low rank (rank 188-197 for 99% of energy).
   All three available mechanisms lose, by 6-7x.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. WHY MoE EXPERT TENSORS ARE NOT IN THIS MATRIX -- AND WHY THAT MATTERS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The proposal is about MoE. The model measured here is DENSE. A real MoE model
was located and parsed:

  F:\OllamaModels\blobs\sha256-764636b0...  96.9 GB  arch=deepseek4  43 layers
     ffn_gate_inp=43  ffn_up_exps=43  ffn_down_exps=43  ffn_gate_exps=43
  F:\OllamaModels\blobs\sha256-948255a1...  89.4 GB  arch=laguna     47 layers
     same MoE tensor structure

Both parse cleanly (1328 tensors, all offsets in bounds, all blocks aligned).
But their EXPERT tensors are NOT Q4_K or Q6_K. The unknown type IDs were
identified by counting and reporting them rather than guessing a geometry:

  UNKNOWN_TYPE_IDS: 17, 18, 26, 39
    17 = IQ2_XXS    18 = IQ2_XS    26 = IQ4_XS    39 = MXFP4

THESE CARRY CODEBOOKS. An i-quant decode is a codebook lookup, and a wrong
codebook index does not fail -- it produces a plausible matrix whose spectral
structure is an ARTEFACT of the wrong codebook. That is precisely the failure the
stationarity check was built to catch, and stationarity is weaker evidence
against a codebook error than against a scale-index error.

So the i-quant expert decode is deliberately NOT implemented. Guessing three
codebooks to produce a rank curve would be exactly the fabrication this project
has produced repeatedly, and it would be invisible: the output would look like
the other eleven rows.

CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY:
  The negative result is established for dense Q4_K and Q6_K. It is NOT
  established for MoE expert tensors. Anyone citing this receipt to close the
  MoE case is overclaiming.

  There is also a substantive reason to expect a DIFFERENT answer for experts:
  experts are quantised with i-quants / MXFP4 precisely because they must fit a
  much smaller budget per expert than a dense projection. A base that coarse
  has a LARGER delta, and therefore plausibly a MORE structured one -- outliers
  and a heavy tail concentrate energy in few directions. That is a hypothesis,
  and it is testable, and it is the one measurement that would change the
  verdict.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. DEFECTS FOUND WHILE EXTENDING THE TOOL
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
D1  GGML BOOL is ONE byte, not four. Every subsequent metadata key
    desynchronised. The parser now records the key/index/offset it was reading
    when the cursor went bad.
D2  The failure diagnostic was packed into the check's own format string behind
    ternaries and printed garbage on SUCCESS (kv=612120512030816), because a
    std::to_string temporary's c_str() was fed to %s. Diagnostics now print
    separately, only on failure.
D3  "blk.0.attn_q" resolved to blk.0.attn_q.bias -- a 1-D F32 bias -- because
    exact-name matching was missing. Every geometry expectation was being
    checked against the wrong tensor.
D4  R was 64, which penalises low-rank and would have made the rank curve an
    artefact of the SAMPLE HEIGHT rather than of the delta. Raised to 256.
D5  windows.h defines min/max as MACROS, rewriting std::max into C2589 rather
    than failing at the call. NOMINMAX added ahead of the include.
D6  The liveness check gated on distinct_bytes > 8 and failed on an F32 bias
    whose low bytes legitimately take few values. Replaced by the condition it
    actually stood in for (all-zero block).
D7  Unknown ggml types were counted but not NAMED, so the MoE expert tensors
    were simply "TYPE_UNSUPPORTED" with no way to act on it. Now prints the IDs.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. WHAT WOULD CHANGE THE VERDICT
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. A VALIDATED i-quant decode (IQ2_XXS / IQ2_XS / IQ4_XS) for the deepseek4 or
     laguna expert tensors, then this same matrix on experts. THIS is the
     measurement that matters. A correct codebook needs its own stationarity and
     value-distribution validation, and its results must NOT be produced until
     that validation passes.
  2. MXFP4 (id 39) on experts -- block 32, 17 bytes, E8M0 scale plus fp4 codes,
     no codebook. Cheaper to validate than the i-quants.
  3. A per-tensor rather than per-256x256-window low-rank fit, to separate "the
     tensor is low rank" from "this window is low rank".
  4. Q5_K / Q8_K, which have better bases still; the trend across Q4_K -> Q6_K
     suggests more base precision lowers entropy but does not rescue rank.


